I actually like MAGMA as a name because it naturally fits the geological metaphor you’ve been building.

If it were an acronym, one possible expansion could be:

MAGMA
Meta-Accumulated Generative Memory Architecture

or

MAGMA
Multi-Anchor Generative Meaning Architecture

But even without forcing an acronym, “magma” already carries useful semantics:

* Molten — ideas are still fluid and can combine.
* Pressure — compression produces transformation.
* Differentiation — as magma cools, different minerals crystallize; as ideas stabilize, different concepts emerge.
* Strata — repeated eruptions create layers that later become readable history.
* Hidden interior — much of the structure exists beneath the surface.

That maps nicely to the abstraction you’ve been describing:

Experience
      ↓
Accumulation
      ↓
MAGMA
(latent compressed substrate)
      ↓
Cooling / Crystallization
      ↓
Language
Code
Theory
Culture
Artifacts

Then, over time:

Crystallized artifacts
          ↓
Sedimentary layers
          ↓
Reconstruction of history
          ↓
Inference of hidden operators

One aspect I especially like is that magma is generative rather than archival. It isn’t just a record of the past; it’s the source material from which new terrain is formed.

That feels consistent with your recurring theme: a substrate that both preserves history and creates future possibilities.